\chapter{Self-hosted authoring and compiler development}
\label{ch:90}
\index{Self-hosted authoring and compiler development}

On1x-authored libraries cover S-expression data, traversal, source/diagnostic structures, schema helpers and native processor logic. A smaller set of runtime/compiler primitives supplies operations those libraries need, including shared values and bounded buffers. The compiler frontend itself still has the C++ implementation discussed in \cref{ch:71} through \cref{ch:78}. Running a native PPX built from On1x exercises a self-hosted component within that larger toolchain.

\section{Add a native authoring operation}
Try to express the operation using reusable On1x modules and existing checked primitives. Test round-tripping and malformed data at the authoring boundary. A new primitive additionally needs a checked declaration, lowering, runtime behavior and lifetime evidence. Retain/release or I/O changes need tests that observe failure and old outputs, not just a success example. Trace the bootstrap from the compiler executable to the built processor and the processor back through public transport. This identifies a dependency cycle before introducing a new one.

\section{Bootstrap an identity processor and test its limits}
Compile the native identity processor's On1x source without any PPX
selected. Keep the generated C as a reference and build the executable.
Now compile that same source through the newly built processor. The
compiler sends a public syntax tree; the processor reads the selected
schema, validates and returns the tree; the compiler admits the returned
syntax and emits C again. The self-host test compares this second C text
with the first. Byte equality is a useful fixed point for this identity
transformation. It does not imply that the native processor parsed the
original On1x source or compiled either version of itself.

To see where a transformation would fit, feed the visitor the data tree
\OnxCodeTerm{(a b)} and print the path at each rewrite callback. The
paths appear as \OnxCodeTerm{(0)}, \OnxCodeTerm{(1)}, then
\OnxCodeTerm{()}: first the two children, then the rebuilt parent.
The components are natural-number symbols, not mutable cursor handles.
Returning a newly constructed node does not start a new traversal of
that node. A transformation that requires another round must account for
that round explicitly rather than depending on accidental repeated visits.

As a negative control, send an identity request with a private HIR
constructor in place of a public type. Although the tree might be valid
S-expression data, it is not a valid tree under the selected schema.
The native processor test expects status 73 and empty standard output.
Keeping diagnostics on the error stream lets the caller distinguish
failure from a complete replacement tree; no partial syntax is an
acceptable identity result. This control exercises the native schema reader
and server protocol together, without needing to expose the compiler's private
value representation to the processor.

